[Music]  welcome to part two of the fundamentals  of kicking series this part is going to  be focused in on pivoting of the foot  and how that relates to correctly  turning your hips into position to  execute certain kicks  [Music]  now each part in this series is designed  to be like a piece of a jigsaw puzzle  that fit together and integrating them  all together at the same time will  improve your kicks exponentially now on  the surface each part of this series  might seem almost too simplistic or kind  of just common sense and you might be  thinking now this is just the series for  like beginners or people who've never  experienced kicking before but that's  seriously not the case in fact if you've  actually been training for a while  you're more likely to have some bad  habits ingrained which are hampering  your progress without you really known  about it so try to keep an open mind  with this and try to identify where you  might be doing some of these things  within your own technique and you'll be  able to improve yourself common sense  does not mean common practice so bear  that in mind  pivoting and turning the hips is  probably the single most common problem  I see martial artists face and a lot of  people become frustrated because they  just can't get their body to do what  they wanted to do I believe the major  reason that people don't really know  about this is that were never actually  taught it by the instructors and that's  likely because they didn't even really  understand it themselves if my own  Taekwondo instructor would just say the  differences between kicks and say this  is a front kick this is a side kick and  that's about as far as it went with no  more detailed explanation I only learnt  this stuff by watching my own footage  back and then comparing it to other  people I wanted to move like and then  adjusting it from there when many people  identify there's a problem with their  kicking they'll set out to change  something thinking that's the underlying  cause but often that's not the case with  regards to this part of kicking 99% of  people will point to what they think is  a lack of flexibility being the cause of  why they're not able to move in the way  they want to but overwhelmingly a bad  pivot which leads to the hips not being  able to open correctly is actually the  cause which just leads to a lackluster  kick at the end this would be like  having a problem with a movie that  you're watching at the cinema and your  solution is to yell at the screen in  front of you hoping it will change the  result of that is completely frivolous  because the real origin is coming from  the projector room and by the time it  hits the screen it's already a done deal  so now let's talk about the two most  predominant positions of the hips when  kicking and these are Square and turned  so the square position is applicable to  things like front kicks and axe kicks  and the turning position is applicable  to the others like side kick or kick and  turning kick the square position means  that our hips face directly towards the  target  turn position is where we face side on  to our target which is achieved through  pivoting on the standing foot correctly  pivoting on the standing foot is a  paramount importance to getting your  hips turned into the correct position to  execute an effective kick this is  something that I believe is grossly  misunderstood by many martial artists  and as a consequence overlooked meaning  that their kicks never really improve as  mentioned before many people believe a  lack of flexibility is the reason behind  their poor kicking technique but  overwhelmingly it is because of bad  pivoting and if you continue to do that  not only will you ingrain it into your  muscle memory and the kicks won't be  anywhere near as effective as they could  be you can also grind the joints of the  legs together and risk injury as well  the best way to illustrate this point is  by talking about the angle at which a  kick comes in toward its target so if  this is your centerline here and say if  you're doing a turning kick that comes  in from the side like this now if you  are kicking correctly however you kick  and whatever height you kick out the  kick should come in at the same angle  each time the hallmark of a poor pivot  and bad alignment of the hips is where  the kick starts low okay and then as it  goes up the angle starts to go upwards  until they're almost performing a full  kick when it's actually supposed to be a  turning kick so as you can see here this  is starting off okay but as the angle  goes up the kick gets weaker and this is  a really weak kicking angle here as  opposed to turning the hips correctly  look at the alignment of the body it's  completely different and even at the  highest point of the kick it's still a  nice strong kick so the way we want to  pivot for the turning of the hips  correctly is by fully turning the foot  away from your target so that the heel  is facing towards it you don't  necessarily need to do that to get the  right hip position at a low angle for  instance but it's a good habit to get  into doing whatever height you kick out  so that you'll naturally do it when  kicking higher and this is the way I do  it  the key to really dynamic kicking  technique is to get into that pivoted  position as fast as you can  the faster you're able to get into that  position with your hips aligned then the  more explosive your kick is going to be  with kicking all of the energy starts  with our feet as we pivot then travels  up the body to its apex where the kick  is extended you might want to imagine  this in terms of the metaphor of when  you like whip a towel out the stored  energy is transferred from your hand  through the towel to the end point where  it's finally discharged so by remaining  very relaxed to start with you're going  to use them the muscles of your legs  combine with your hands to turn your  body into that correctly pivoted  position that'll make the kick can have  just happen all by itself after that and  one of the best ways I've found to  actually do this when you get  comfortable with it is to almost jump  yourself into that position so the foot  goes from exactly where you're facing  your target in your fighting stance to  completely turned into the correct  pivoting position which makes the kick  really really powerful and explosive you  see many people kick like this where  they slowly turn the hip into the pivot  which is a good starting point but once  you're familiar with it then you should  begin to speed it all up in a moment I'm  going to close out this video with a  collection of different clips of  different clicking techniques with the  pivot clearly shown to illustrate my  point and perhaps the best way against  practice this is slowly holding on to  something for your balance so that you  can really really focus on that element  that we want to burn into your muscle  memory in this case the pivot and  turning the hips correctly that way you  can really concentrate on that pivot and  practice how it will feel to kick in  this new way while also strengthening  specific muscles that you're going to  need to be able to keep the form at  higher intensity levels I hope you  enjoyed this video guys and I will see  you next time  [Music]  you

